---

<DocsLayout title="Scheduling Policy" toc={toc}>

  <h1>Scheduling Policy</h1>

  <p>
    This page details how Slurm places and orders jobs on polimi-cluster. For the practical summary,
    see <a href={`${base}docs/slurm#queues`}>Choosing a Queue</a> and
    <a href={`${base}docs/slurm#rules`}>Submission Rules</a>.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Order ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="order">Scheduling Order</h2>

  <p>
    A job can start only when resources are free and its limits allow it. Among eligible jobs, Slurm
    first looks at the <strong>partition tier</strong>, then at the <strong>job score</strong> within
    the same tier. The two are separate: a high-scoring <code>bulk</code> job does not outrank a
    lower-scoring <code>normal</code> job.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Partition</th><th>Tier</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>debug</code></td><td>300</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>normal</code></td><td>200</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>cpu</code></td><td>150</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>bulk</code></td><td>50</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    <strong>Backfilling</strong> lets a lower-priority job start early if it fits in a free gap
    without delaying higher-priority jobs, which is why a realistic <code>--time</code> pays off.
    Preemption and reservations also affect start times.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Placement ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="placement">GPU Placement</h2>

  <p>
    The VRAM declaration (<code>--comment=vram=…</code>, per GPU) sets the smallest model that fits,
    based on each card's usable capacity:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Declared VRAM per GPU</th><th>Smallest fitting model</th><th>Model name</th><th>GPUs per node</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1 – 23034 MiB</td><td>NVIDIA L4</td><td><code>l4</code></td><td>8</td></tr>
      <tr><td>23035 – 24576 MiB</td><td>Quadro RTX 6000</td><td><code>quadro_rtx_6000</code></td><td>8</td></tr>
      <tr><td>24577 – 49140 MiB</td><td>NVIDIA RTX A6000</td><td><code>nvidia_rtx_a6000</code></td><td>6</td></tr>
      <tr><td>49141 – 97887 MiB</td><td>NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000</td><td><code>rtx_pro_6000</code></td><td>6</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Examples: <code>vram=24G</code> selects a Quadro RTX 6000, <code>vram=48G</code> needs an
    RTX PRO 6000, and <code>vram=96G</code> exceeds every card.
  </p>

  <p>
    How each queue uses these models is described in <a href={`${base}docs/slurm#gpu`}>GPU Jobs</a>
    and <a href={`${base}docs/slurm#bulk`}>Long Jobs on bulk</a>.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Preemption ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="preemption">Preemption</h2>

  <p>
    Only <code>bulk</code> jobs are preempted. A preempted job is requeued and its script runs again
    from the beginning.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>A pending job in…</th><th>can preempt a running <code>bulk</code> job?</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>normal</code></td><td>✓ Always, also when the <code>bulk</code> job is on the model it needs</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>debug</code></td><td>✓ Always, as above</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>bulk</code></td><td>✓ Only if it needs a larger model than the running job, that job is borrowing it, and the pending job has a higher score</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>cpu</code></td><td>✗ CPU jobs are outside GPU preemption</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <ul>
    <li>Running <code>normal</code> and <code>debug</code> jobs are never preempted by this policy. <code>debug</code> waits for running <code>normal</code> jobs.</li>
    <li>Between <code>bulk</code> jobs, the account type gives a preference, but fair-share and waiting time can outweigh it. A larger VRAM request alone does not guarantee preemption.</li>
    <li>A request for a specific model counts as needing that model, even with a small VRAM declaration.</li>
    <li><code>bulk</code> jobs are made requeueable automatically. On <code>normal</code>, <code>--requeue</code> / <code>--no-requeue</code> still control restarts after other events, such as node failures.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- ── Score ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="score">Priority Score</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="text"
    filename="job score"
    code={`score ≈ 100 000 × QoS / 3000                     QoS priority (table below)
      +  10 000 × FairShare                       0…1, from your recent usage
      +   5 000 × min(waiting time / 7 days, 1)   age of the pending job`}
  />

  <p>
    The QoS is assigned automatically from your account type and the chosen partition.
    Scores are integers, so values are approximate after rounding.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>QoS priority</th><th>PhD</th><th>MSc</th><th>External</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>debug</code></td><td>3000</td><td>2500</td><td>2000</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>normal</code></td><td>2000</td><td>1500</td><td>1000</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>cpu</code></td><td>1500</td><td>1000</td><td>750</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>bulk</code></td><td>1000</td><td>500</td><td>400</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <!-- ── Billing ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="billing">Billing & Usage</h2>

  <p>
    "Billing" is usage accounting for scheduling, not a monetary charge. Each running job has a
    billing value computed from its allocated resources and partition:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="text"
    filename="billing"
    code={`billing = ⌊ Σ  allocated amount × weight ⌋        (per resource, per partition)
usage   = billing × running hours                (billing-unit-hours)
decay   = usage recorded d days ago keeps 2^(−d/7) of its weight`}
  />

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Resource</th><th><code>debug</code> / <code>bulk</code></th><th><code>normal</code></th><th><code>cpu</code></th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Each CPU</td><td>0.32</td><td>0.32</td><td>0.32</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Memory</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Any GPU (generic)</td><td>16</td><td>20</td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
      <tr><td>+ L4 / Quadro RTX 6000</td><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
      <tr><td>+ RTX A6000</td><td>12</td><td>15</td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
      <tr><td>+ RTX PRO 6000</td><td>32</td><td>40</td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    A GPU is billed with both the generic and the model weight: a Quadro RTX 6000 in
    <code>bulk</code> counts <code>16 + 4 = 20</code>. Usage halves after 7 days and drops to a quarter after 14; it
    never resets all at once. It is recalculated every 5 minutes, including for running jobs.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Fair-share ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="fairshare">Fair-Share</h2>

  <p>
    Fair-share uses Slurm's <strong>Fair Tree</strong> algorithm. Accounts have a relative target
    share of the cluster, and users within an account have equal shares:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Account</th><th>Relative share</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>PhD</td><td>100</td></tr>
      <tr><td>MSc</td><td>50</td></tr>
      <tr><td>External</td><td>20</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Shares are targets, not reserved resources or GPU-hour allowances. At each level of the
    account tree:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="text"
    filename="fair tree"
    code={`LevelFS   = (share / sum of sibling shares) ÷ (usage / sum of sibling usage)
            > 1 → under-using,  < 1 → over-using
FairShare = rank / N      users ranked by LevelFS, account first, then user (best rank = N)`}
  />

  <p>
    Because FairShare depends on a <strong>rank</strong>, there is no fixed "points lost per GPU-hour":
    extra usage lowers your priority only if it moves you down the ranking relative to other users.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Example ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="example">Worked Example</h2>

  <p>
    A PhD user runs one Quadro RTX 6000 with 16 CPUs for 24 hours:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th><code>normal</code></th><th><code>bulk</code></th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Tier</td><td>200</td><td>50</td></tr>
      <tr><td>QoS score contribution</td><td>≈ 66 667</td><td>≈ 33 333</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Preemptible</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Billing</td><td>⌊16 × 0.32 + 20 + 5⌋ = 30</td><td>⌊16 × 0.32 + 16 + 4⌋ = 25</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Usage after 24 h (before decay)</td><td><strong>720</strong></td><td><strong>600</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Same hardware, same time, but <code>normal</code> records 20% more usage. In exchange it is
    scheduled sooner and cannot be preempted. Switching partitions later does not erase that history.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Limits ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>

  <p>
    All limits are per user and per partition, and shared across all GPU models. A limit of
    "–" means no explicit cap: available resources and the other limits still apply.
  </p>

  <h3>Maximum runtime</h3>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Partition</th><th>PhD</th><th>MSc / External</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>debug</code></td><td>2 hours</td><td>1 hour</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>normal</code></td><td>24 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>cpu</code></td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>bulk</code></td><td>5 days</td><td>5 days</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Jobs: running / running + pending</h3>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Partition</th><th>PhD</th><th>MSc</th><th>External</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>debug</code></td><td>1 / –</td><td>1 / 5</td><td>1 / 5</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>normal</code></td><td>– / –</td><td>1 / 10</td><td>1 / 20</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>bulk</code></td><td>– / –</td><td>– / 20</td><td>20 / 200</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>cpu</code></td><td>– / –</td><td>4 / 30</td><td>10 / 100</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Over the running limit, extra jobs wait; over the running + pending limit, new submissions are
    rejected. Each array task counts as one job.
  </p>

  <h3>GPUs in use at once</h3>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Partition</th><th>PhD</th><th>MSc</th><th>External</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>debug</code></td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>normal</code></td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>3</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>bulk</code></td><td>–</td><td>8</td><td>8</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>cpu</code></td><td colspan="3">GPU requests not allowed</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    GPU limits are shared by all your running jobs in the same partition, whatever the model. For
    example, a PhD user on <code>normal</code> can run three one-GPU jobs, or one two-GPU job plus
    one one-GPU job, but never four GPUs at once. Limits apply separately per partition: an MSc user
    can use a GPU on <code>normal</code> and up to eight on <code>bulk</code> at the same time.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Check ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="check">Check Your Values</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    filename="polimi-cluster"
    code={`# Your account, shares, and allowed QoS
sacctmgr -P show assoc where user="$USER" format=Account,User,Fairshare,DefaultQOS,QOS

# Your current decayed usage and fair-share
sshare -l -u "$USER"

# Score components of your pending jobs
sprio -l -u "$USER"

# Your jobs of the last 7 days, with billing=... in AllocTRES
sacct -X -u "$USER" -S now-7days -P --format=JobID,Partition,QOS,Elapsed,State,AllocTRES

# Configured QoS limits
sacctmgr -P show qos format=Name,MaxJobsPU,MaxSubmitPU,MaxTRESPU,MaxWall`}
  />

</DocsLayout>
